<p>Hungary’s opposition Tisza party, led by Péter Magyar, defeated Prime Minister Viktor Orbán in Sunday’s parliamentary election, ending Orbán’s 16 years in power after he publicly conceded a result he called “painful” but clear. Multiple outlets reported that Tisza was on course for or had secured a two-thirds parliamentary majority, giving Magyar a strong mandate as Hungary heads for its first change of government since 2010. The election drew record participation, with turnout reaching 77.8%, the highest since the fall of communism, underscoring the scale of public engagement in a vote widely seen as Hungary’s most consequential in years. Across the coverage, voters’ concerns centered on corruption, weak economic growth, strained public services, and Orbán’s years of conflict with the European Union, while Magyar campaigned on restoring EU ties, reducing ties with Moscow, and pursuing domestic reforms. The result immediately drew attention across Europe and beyond because Orbán had long been one of the EU’s most disruptive leaders on issues including rule-of-law disputes and support for Ukraine, while also maintaining close political ties with President Trump and Russian President Vladimir Putin. European leaders quickly welcomed the outcome as a chance for Hungary to reset relations with Brussels, even as analysts noted that Magyar, a conservative and former insider, will now face the much harder task of turning a sweeping victory into institutional change.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/3VwoGtRU74btt5T2sZatPOai2L1irukOwACvHNPv_dXuwgDcQSCeidYx9HmHTFfj-zw37gqrM0bME1CmI6PZhSWOyu6AGbTqvqakxS2_4I0FInaGfwvjaqhWLlBXBe3vZIfUwYE1wjIlQOO6PLZ_DWAEFJtTgBY0bGdNQ9aCzvTZyyqM5KZNyOxJamDtzTZwxA8KNHYYdKPyJJvJIOqZVkfS_DVtF8hDKeMS447ImWqtschKgqMp7Oj6jECT7pLamvZvB41oDzX08kbqlg' alt='A man waves a Hungarian flag as he celebrates in the streets after the announcement of partial results of the Hungarian parliamentary election in Budapest, Sunday, April 12.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Seat split: With votes nearly fully counted, Tisza was reported at about 138 of 199 seats, while Orbán’s Fidesz was projected to win 55 and the far-right Our Homeland party six.</li><li>Concession speed: Orbán conceded less than three hours after polls closed, and Magyar later said the prime minister phoned him to congratulate the winning party.</li><li>Street mood: Budapest saw large celebrations after the concession, with supporters gathering along the Danube and in central public spaces as the result signaled a rare peaceful transfer of power after a long incumbency.</li><li>US ripple: The result was cast as a blow to MAGA-style politics abroad after President Trump backed Orbán and Vice President JD Vance reportedly campaigned for him in the final days.</li></ul><blockquote>The election results, though not yet final, are clear and understandable; for us, they are painful but unambiguous. - Viktor Orbán</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Péter Magyar: Magyar celebrated the result as a vote for “a free Hungary” and called on Orbán loyalists in state institutions to resign, framing the win as a mandate for deeper change than a simple cabinet swap. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/hungarian-election-2026-the-winners-and-losers/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>Viktor Orbán: Orbán accepted the result and said Fidesz would serve Hungary from the opposition, signaling a formal concession after the party’s defeat. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/4/12/ive-congratulated-the-victorious-party-viktor-orban-concedes'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>European Commission: Ursula von der Leyen posted that “Hungary has chosen Europe” just 17 minutes after Orbán conceded, underscoring how quickly one of his fiercest Brussels rivals moved to mark the outcome. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/viktor-orban-ursula-von-der-leyen-rejoices-at-crushing-defeat/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>Hungarian voters: Some voters interviewed at victory gatherings said they arrived hopeful for change and optimistic that the election could bring a different political atmosphere after years of tension. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1991135/viktor-orban-concedes-painful-defeat-to-conservative-peter-magyar-in-hungary-polls'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-orbans-far-right-party-swept-out-of-power-after-16-year-reign/'>Orban’s far-right party swept out of power after 16-year reign in Hungary</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.rferl.org/a/hungarian-opposition-leader-magyar-landslide-victory-orban/33730633.html'>Hungarian Opposition Leader Magyar Scores Landslide Win To Oust Orban</a> - rferl.org</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/a9b635f0-6fff-41e5-8e27-245de4500863'>Hungary’s Orbán concedes defeat as opposition secures landslide win</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-04-12-0700/elections-record-turnout-expected-parliamentary-election-hungary-gets-under-way'>Record turnout expected as parliamentary election in Hungary gets under way</a> - allsides.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/hungarian-prime-minister-viktor-orbn-concedes-defeat-after-131965262'>Hungarian Prime Minister Orbán is ejected after 16 years in a European electoral earthquake</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/04/12/peter-magyar-says-viktor-orban-has-conceded-victory-in-hungary-election_6752347_4.html'>Péter Magyar wins two-thirds parliamentary majority in earthquake Hungary election</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.economist.com/europe/2026/04/12/peter-magyar-topples-illiberal-hero-viktor-orban-in-hungary'>Peter Magyar topples illiberal hero Viktor Orban in Hungary</a> - economist.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/hungarys-viktor-orban-a-trump-ally-loses-polls-after-16-years-in-power-11348436#publisher=ne